# Authoritative product amendment — Portable Remote + Remote Control surface

This amendment is authoritative over any conflicting wording earlier in this issue. In particular, it supersedes the installed-worker-first assumption and the statement that Python 3.12 may remain a normal endpoint prerequisite.

## A. Portable/no-install is the primary endpoint mode

The primary onsite/customer-machine workflow is a **portable worker**, comparable operationally to a portable support utility:

```text
Remote Control tab in Orion
    ↓ download portable worker
customer machine
    ↓ run directly, no installer
enter/display name + one-time pairing code
    ↓ outbound HTTPS/WSS only
Orion
```

Requirements:
- no MSI/setup wizard;
- no Program Files installation;
- no PATH mutation;
- no Start Menu entry;
- no Windows service;
- no scheduled task;
- no registry persistence except unavoidable OS/runtime bookkeeping outside Orion's control;
- no autostart;
- no inbound listener/firewall rule;
- no Python/Node/npm prerequisite on the customer machine for the normal portable artifact;
- no E5/model/UI dependency;
- visible foreground application/status while connected;
- explicit Disconnect/Exit;
- worker-created temporary state is removed on clean exit in temporary mode;
- never attempt to erase Windows/EDR/audit/prefetch/download history or conceal that the program ran.

The portable worker may run from Downloads, a temporary folder, network share, or removable media when OS policy permits. Do not attempt to bypass AppLocker, Defender, EDR, execution policy, UAC, software restriction policy, or customer controls.

A persistent installed/service mode may also be implemented for machines the Orion owner controls, but it is secondary and optional. It must use the same protocol/capability implementation rather than a separate worker.

## B. Temporary session mode is first-class and default

Portable startup defaults to a temporary endpoint session:
- operator chooses a bounded display name, e.g. `KH-A-PC01`;
- operator supplies a short-lived one-time pairing code generated by Orion;
- long-lived endpoint credential is **not written to disk** in temporary mode;
- device credential/session state lives only in worker process memory;
- closing/exiting the worker invalidates or expires that temporary device session server-side;
- reconnect after process exit requires a new pairing code;
- temporary endpoint records are visibly marked temporary;
- provide an authenticated `End & forget` operation that revokes the session and removes endpoint-owned Orion metadata/history according to existing persistence safety rules.

Persistent mode is explicit:
- owner chooses “Remember this device” / equivalent;
- credential is stored only in worker-owned protected local state;
- revocation remains available from Orion.

Temporary and persistent modes use the same worker binary.

## C. Self-contained worker artifacts

Produce self-contained portable artifacts suitable for running without installing Python:

```text
OrionRemote-<version>-windows-x64.zip
  └── OrionRemote.exe

OrionRemote-<version>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
  └── orion-remote
```

Exact archive/internal names may follow repository conventions, but normal endpoint use must not require Python, pip, Node, npm, Git, or a source checkout.

Use a deterministic maintained bundling approach. Avoid obfuscation, injection, packer tricks, hidden persistence, or behavior intended to evade endpoint protection. If a single-file executable materially harms reliability/security/AV behavior, a small self-contained portable directory is acceptable, but it must still be zero-install and run directly after extraction.

Release manifests/checksums bind worker artifacts to Orion version + source SHA. Existing full Orion release artifacts remain separate.

## D. Top-level Remote Control web surface

Add a first-class top-level UI surface separate from Chat and Project, conceptually:

```text
Chat | Projects | Remote Control
```

The Remote Control landing page must include:
- `Download Windows portable worker`;
- `Download Linux portable worker`;
- worker version/compatibility information;
- SHA-256/checksum display or verified download metadata;
- `Create temporary pairing code`;
- bounded code expiry/countdown;
- connected/known endpoint list.

Endpoint rows/cards expose only safe metadata, for example:
- display name;
- endpoint ref;
- temporary/persistent;
- online/offline;
- OS/platform/version;
- worker version;
- architecture;
- safe CPU/RAM summary when recently observed;
- display/desktop availability;
- browser capability;
- last seen.

Clicking a connected endpoint opens a dedicated endpoint workspace, conceptually:

```text
KH-A-PC01 · Windows 11 · Online

[Chat] [Desktop] [Files] [Processes] [Browser] [Connection]
```

Do not require the user to know IP addresses or endpoint UUIDs in ordinary UI operation.

## E. Worker download from the Remote Control tab

The web surface must be able to offer the worker artifact directly for the running Orion version.

Preferred contract:
- release workflow publishes both portable worker platform artifacts;
- Orion has versioned trusted worker-artifact metadata (filename, platform, version, source SHA, SHA-256);
- Remote Control tab resolves the compatible artifact for the running server version;
- browser click starts the normal authenticated download flow or a verified release-asset download;
- no credential/pairing token is embedded in the worker binary or download URL;
- pairing remains a separate one-time step;
- source/dev builds where no portable artifact exists show an explicit “artifact unavailable for this build” state rather than fabricating a download.

If serving artifacts through Orion itself is cleaner, the payload must be integrity-verified and the route browser-authenticated. If linking to a release asset is cleaner, use the exact running-version artifact and surface its expected checksum.

## F. Per-endpoint Device Chat is a separate product surface

Every paired endpoint has a dedicated **Device Chat** owned by that endpoint workspace.

Product semantics:
- Device Chat is not listed in ordinary Chat;
- Device Chat is not a Project conversation;
- it has no `project_id`;
- it does not inherit Project instructions/documents;
- it does not inherit ordinary Chat attachments;
- ordinary Chat/Project history is not replayed into it;
- each endpoint has its own persistent Device Chat history unless the endpoint is explicitly forgotten/deleted;
- Device Chat remains visible while an endpoint is offline, but endpoint tool calls return the normal bounded offline failure;
- `End & forget` may remove the endpoint-owned Device Chat after explicit confirmation.

Implementation invariant:
- **do not create a second agent/runtime**;
- Device Chat must reuse the same canonical `ChatRuntime`, model backend, registry, ToolRunner, mutation authorization and timeline machinery;
- represent it as a distinct persisted session/surface kind bound deterministically to exactly one `endpoint_id`;
- extend `RuntimeScope`/context with the bound endpoint identity in a server-owned field;
- endpoint identity is never model-selected authority.

Endpoint binding:
- within Device Chat, endpoint operations are constrained to the bound endpoint;
- a model call must not escape to a different endpoint by supplying another `target_ref`;
- the active endpoint's safe display name/platform/online/capability summary is deterministic context;
- full credentials/transport metadata never enter model context.

Device Chat may still use ordinary non-endpoint tools (calculator, Internet, etc.) through the canonical registry when useful, but it has **no Project/RAG scope unless a future explicit design adds one**. Endpoint output remains untrusted data.

## G. Device Chat permissions

Device Chat has its own persisted mutation mode, independent of ordinary Chat/Project conversations:
- `read_only`;
- `confirm`;
- `auto` subject to all server/worker policy ceilings.

Default to the repository's safe conversation default unless existing UX rules establish a stricter endpoint-specific default.

Manual Remote Desktop input remains a direct authenticated user-control channel and is not synthesized as Device Chat model authorization. Device Chat model mutations still go through normal `ToolRunner` authorization.

## H. Device Chat APIs and routing

Provide protected APIs/streaming routes needed for the endpoint-bound chat surface while reusing ordinary Chat request/timeline contracts where possible.

Requirements:
- browser-authenticated in Remote Access mode;
- state-changing calls require exact trusted Origin;
- endpoint/session binding enforced server-side;
- no request body/query may select principal/workspace/project authority;
- Remote Access route-policy inventory explicitly classifies all new routes;
- unknown API routes remain 404.

Avoid duplicating the entire Chat API if a thin endpoint-bound session/resource layer can reuse it safely.

## I. Remote Control UI tests

Add deterministic UI/backend coverage for:
- Remote Control top-level navigation;
- Windows/Linux worker download actions and unavailable-development-build state;
- pairing-code creation/expiry;
- endpoint list safe OS/online metadata;
- selecting an endpoint opens its workspace;
- Device Chat history isolated from ordinary Chat;
- Device Chat has no Project scope/documents/attachments;
- endpoint-bound chat cannot target another endpoint;
- independent mutation mode persists;
- offline endpoint gives bounded tool failure without corrupting chat;
- temporary endpoint Exit/End & forget lifecycle;
- Desktop/Files/Processes/Browser tabs use the selected endpoint only;
- no credentials/pairing secrets in browser storage, URLs, rendered metadata or logs.

## J. Completion report additions

In addition to the existing 27 completion-report items, explicitly report:
- portable artifact format and whether it requires any endpoint prerequisite;
- temporary vs persistent worker state/credential behavior;
- exact cleanup performed on portable Exit and what OS/security telemetry Orion deliberately does **not** erase;
- Remote Control download UX and artifact integrity path;
- Device Chat persistence/session schema;
- proof that Device Chat is isolated from ordinary Chat/Project while reusing the same `ChatRuntime`;
- endpoint-binding enforcement tests;
- Remote Control UI test results.
