# 15. Lifecycle and concurrency

Endpoint manager belongs to the existing application composition root.

Startup:
1. load persistence/config;
2. initialize endpoint manager;
3. register stable endpoint tool family;
4. freeze canonical registry;
5. start HTTP/WebSocket application.

Shutdown:
- reject new endpoint dispatch;
- cancel/drain reads;
- mark in-flight ambiguous mutations appropriately;
- close desktop/browser relay state;
- close worker WebSockets;
- close application cleanly.

Concurrency v1:
- bounded per-endpoint request queue;
- at most one endpoint mutation executing at a time per endpoint;
- reads may run concurrently only with explicit safe bound;
- desktop stream has its own bounded frame queue and drops stale frames rather than growing memory;
- browser automation serializes mutations for one browser context.

No busy polling.

Tests need injectable clocks/timeouts and deterministic fake workers.

